  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The offense never arrived. Three hits and ten strikeouts against Minnesota is not a close game masquerading as one. It's a team that showed up to pitch and nothing else. Brady Basso gave them 2.1 scoreless innings of clean work, then watched the bullpen hold for 5.2 more, and none of it mattered because you don't win 2\u20130 games when you can't produce a single run. They dug themselves into that hole in the sixth inning when Minnesota scored twice off Mason Barnett and the bats stayed silent through the ninth. This wasn't a pitching failure. This was an offense that went missing.",
    "what_this_means": "The problem isn't new. It's the same one that's been sitting in the middle of the lineup all season. They had nine plate appearances with runners in scoring position and converted zero of them. Inning three: two runners on, nothing. Inning five: three runners on, nothing. Inning eight: three more runners on, nothing. That's eleven runners left on base across four separate innings, and the game was decided in the one inning where Minnesota actually got something done. Jeff McNeil went 2-for-4, which is something, but it's not enough when the rest of the lineup is a vacuum. A shutout loss is bad. A shutout loss when your pitching earned the right to win is worse. They fell back nine games in the division. The gap doesn't close on nights like this.",
    "what_to_watch": "Connor Prielipp takes the mound tonight for Minnesota with a 5.00 ERA. Jeffrey Springs starts for Oakland. If the bats don't answer in the rematch, the collapse in Target Field gets another chapter tomorrow.",
